<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndebele Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ndebele</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ndebele Kingdom was a state established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a> on the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> around 1840. Its arrival from the south completed the military occupation of the former heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>. The kingdom drew in part on earlier plateau political precedents and later fell to British colonial forces in the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom.jpg" alt="Ndebele Kingdom" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scanned by Ulamm, modified by Fvasconcellos · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matabeleland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lewanika led raids against the Ila and Tonga to the east in the 1880s, bringing back cattle and captives, but <b>Ndebele</b> raids from the south and enemies at his own court led him by the late 1880s to seek British protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rozvi political precedents also informed successor arrangements on the plateau, including the <b>Ndebele</b> kingdom established by Mzilikazi from around 1840.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s rise reshaped southern Africa: groups fleeing Zulu expansion or breaking away from it founded new states, among them the <b>Ndebele Kingdom</b> of Mzilikazi, while the Gaza kingdom of Soshangane emerged from the defeated Ndwandwe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Others left: Mzilikazi, a general of Shaka’s, fled and built the <b>Ndebele</b> kingdom, and Mpande left Dingane with 17,000 followers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Zimbabwe adopted a Shona and <b>Ndebele</b> version, &quot;Ishe Komborera Africa&quot;, at independence in 1980 and replaced it in 1994; Namibia used the hymn at independence in March 1990 and replaced it in 1991 with &quot;Namibia, Land of the Brave&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Shosholoza&quot; is a traditional Nguni work song of the mine workers of southern Africa, in a mix of <b>Ndebele</b> and Zulu words into which singers bring other South African languages.</blockquote><a href="/songs/shosholoza">Shosholoza</a></li></ul></section>
